  "ideasSmoothFillTitle": "Streak-free fill",
  "ideasSmoothFillSummary": "Fill a square with one marker without visible streaks.",
  "ideasSmoothFillPractice": "Even, streak-free coverage, the base of every marker drawing.",
  "ideasSmoothFillSubject": "A 5 × 5 cm square framed with masking tape, then a simple flat shape such as a leaf.",
  "ideasSmoothFillTip": "If the paper starts to pill, stop: it is saturated. Let it dry completely before another layer.",
  "ideasSmoothFillStep1": "Tape a 5 × 5 cm square on marker paper and slip a scrap sheet underneath.",
  "ideasSmoothFillStep2": "Fill the square with the light marker in small overlapping circles, without stopping.",
  "ideasSmoothFillStep3": "Keep a wet edge: always go back over ink that is still shiny, never over dry ink.",
  "ideasSmoothFillStep4": "Let it dry, then add a second layer with the same marker, at right angles to the first.",
  "ideasSmoothFillStep5": "Repeat the square with the darker marker and compare: streaks show more in dark colours.",
  "ideasSmoothFillStep6": "Peel the tape off slowly to reveal a clean edge.",
  "ideasThreeMarkerBlendTitle": "Three-marker blend",
  "ideasThreeMarkerBlendSummary": "Blend a light, a mid and a dark marker of one family on a ball.",
  "ideasThreeMarkerBlendPractice": "Smooth light-to-dark blending within one colour family.",
  "ideasThreeMarkerBlendSubject": "A ball lit from the top left, with its shadow on the table.",
  "ideasThreeMarkerBlendTip": "Pick markers that are close in tone: if the jump between them is too big, a line will show.",
  "ideasThreeMarkerBlendStep1": "Draw a circle in light pencil and mark where the light comes from.",
  "ideasThreeMarkerBlendStep2": "Cover the whole ball with the light marker, leaving a small spot of white paper for the highlight.",
  "ideasThreeMarkerBlendStep3": "While it is still damp, add the mid marker on the shadow half, in curved strokes that follow the shape.",
  "ideasThreeMarkerBlendStep4": "Add the dark marker only along the edge furthest from the light.",
  "ideasThreeMarkerBlendStep5": "Go back over the joins with the mid marker and then with the light one to melt the steps together.",
  "ideasThreeMarkerBlendStep6": "Soften any hard edge around the highlight with the colourless blender.",
  "ideasFlickingTitle": "Flicking strokes",
  "ideasFlickingSummary": "Draw hair or grass with quick strokes that fade out at the end.",
  "ideasFlickingPractice": "Flicking: fast strokes that taper off, to blend without hard edges.",
  "ideasFlickingSubject": "A ponytail or a tuft of grass.",
  "ideasFlickingTip": "Try it on a scrap first: the faster you move, the thinner the tail of the stroke.",
  "ideasFlickingStep1": "Pencil the main locks or blades as simple shapes, without detail.",
  "ideasFlickingStep2": "Cover each shape with the light marker in one flat layer and let it dry.",
  "ideasFlickingStep3": "Flick the mid marker from the roots outwards: press, move fast and lift before the end of the stroke.",
  "ideasFlickingStep4": "Do the same with the dark marker, only from the roots and shorter, where the locks overlap.",
  "ideasFlickingStep5": "Flick the light marker from the tips back towards the roots to soften the change of tone.",
  "ideasFlickingStep6": "Leave a band of the first layer where the hair catches the light.",
  "ideasGrayscaleTitle": "Shades of grey",
  "ideasGrayscaleSummary": "Draw a simple object with one grey family to see how light and shadow build form.",
  "ideasGrayscalePractice": "Seeing light and shadow as steps from light to dark.",
  "ideasGrayscaleSubject": "A mug, an apple or an egg under a single lamp.",
  "ideasGrayscaleTip": "Do not mix warm and cool greys in the same object: pick one family.",
  "ideasGrayscaleStep1": "Pick a simple object and mark in pencil where the light comes from.",
  "ideasGrayscaleStep2": "Leave the highlight as white paper. Never colour over it.",
  "ideasGrayscaleStep3": "Cover the whole object with the lightest grey and let it dry.",
  "ideasGrayscaleStep4": "Add the next grey on the shadow side, blending the edge while it is still wet.",
  "ideasGrayscaleStep5": "Repeat with each darker grey, covering a smaller area each time.",
  "ideasGrayscaleStep6": "Use the darkest grey only for the cast shadow and the contact line under the object.",
  "ideasStickerEffectTitle": "Sticker effect",
  "ideasStickerEffectSummary": "Make a character look like a cut-out sticker with a paper-white border and a coloured background.",
  "ideasStickerEffectPractice": "Keeping the white of the paper on purpose.",
  "ideasStickerEffectSubject": "A cat, a slice of pizza or a cartoon star.",
  "ideasStickerEffectTip": "The white must be the paper. White gel over marker ink looks patchy.",
  "ideasStickerEffectStep1": "Draw your subject, then pencil a second outline 3–5 mm outside it: that is the sticker border.",
  "ideasStickerEffectStep2": "Colour the subject from light to dark without touching the border.",
  "ideasStickerEffectStep3": "Colour the background around the border in one flat colour, so the white edge shows.",
  "ideasStickerEffectStep4": "When it is dry, go over the outside of the border with a black liner or brush pen.",
  "ideasStickerEffectStep5": "Add a grey shadow along one side of the border to lift the sticker off the page.",
  "ideasGlossyCherriesTitle": "Glossy cherries",
  "ideasGlossyCherriesSummary": "Make two cherries shine with a kept highlight and a reflected light.",
  "ideasGlossyCherriesPractice": "Crisp highlights and reflected light, the keys to a shiny surface.",
  "ideasGlossyCherriesSubject": "Two cherries on a stalk, lit from one side.",
  "ideasGlossyCherriesTip": "Shiny surfaces have hard-edged highlights: do not blend them, keep the border crisp.",
  "ideasGlossyCherriesStep1": "Pencil the cherries and the shape of each highlight: a crisp bean shape near the top.",
  "ideasGlossyCherriesStep2": "Cover each cherry with the light marker, going around the highlights so they stay white paper.",
  "ideasGlossyCherriesStep3": "Add the base everywhere except a thin crescent along the bottom edge: that is the reflected light.",
  "ideasGlossyCherriesStep4": "Lay the shadow on the side away from the light and blend it into the base while it is wet.",
  "ideasGlossyCherriesStep5": "Add the darkest marker in a narrow band between the shadow and the reflected light.",
  "ideasGlossyCherriesStep6": "Draw the shadow on the table with the grey and, once dry, add a white gel dot on each highlight.",
  "ideasColoredShadowsTitle": "Coloured shadows",
  "ideasColoredShadowsSummary": "Shade the same lemon twice, once with grey and once with violet, and compare.",
  "ideasColoredShadowsPractice": "Choosing shadow colours that keep a drawing alive.",
  "ideasColoredShadowsSubject": "Two lemons side by side on a white table.",
  "ideasColoredShadowsTip": "A shadow often leans towards the opposite of the light: violet under yellow, blue under orange.",
  "ideasColoredShadowsStep1": "Draw two identical lemons side by side, with the light coming from the same side.",
  "ideasColoredShadowsStep2": "Colour both with the yellow, leaving a small highlight of white paper.",
  "ideasColoredShadowsStep3": "On the first lemon, shade the dark side and the shadow on the table with the grey.",
  "ideasColoredShadowsStep4": "On the second, do the same with the violet, gently: one layer is enough.",
  "ideasColoredShadowsStep5": "Go over the edge of the second shadow with the yellow to blend it in.",
  "ideasColoredShadowsStep6": "Look at them from a distance: the violet shadow looks brighter and more natural next to the yellow.",
  "ideasStainedGlassTitle": "Stained glass",
  "ideasStainedGlassSummary": "Colour the glass panes with simple gradients and add the lead lines at the end.",
  "ideasStainedGlassPractice": "Clean gradients in small areas; the black lines hide uneven edges.",
  "ideasStainedGlassSubject": "A round window with a flower or a sun in the middle.",
  "ideasStainedGlassTip": "If you draw the black lines first, use pigment ink that resists alcohol: ordinary ink smears under the marker.",
  "ideasStainedGlassStep1": "Pencil the window and divide it into panes of different sizes.",
  "ideasStainedGlassStep2": "Colour the panes of the main colour first, each one as a gradient: darker at one edge, lighter in the middle.",
  "ideasStainedGlassStep3": "Do the same with the second and third colours, letting each pane dry before colouring the one next to it.",
  "ideasStainedGlassStep4": "Use the accent in just a few small panes so they stand out.",
  "ideasStainedGlassStep5": "When everything is dry, go over the borders with thick lines of black liner or brush pen.",
  "ideasStainedGlassStep6": "Add a small stroke of white gel on a few panes to suggest the shine of the glass.",
  "ideasSwatchChartTitle": "Your own colour chart",
  "ideasSwatchChartSummary": "Test how each marker looks with one, two and three layers.",
  "ideasSwatchChartPractice": "Knowing how your markers really look on paper, not on the cap.",
  "ideasSwatchChartSubject": "A grid with one row per marker, grouped by colour family.",
  "ideasSwatchChartTip": "Make the chart on the paper you really use: the same marker changes a lot from one paper to another.",
  "ideasSwatchChartStep1": "Draw a grid on the paper you usually use: one row per marker and four squares per row.",
  "ideasSwatchChartStep2": "Fill the first square with one layer, the second with two and the third with three, letting each layer dry.",
  "ideasSwatchChartStep3": "In the fourth square, lay one layer and lift part of the ink with the colourless blender.",
  "ideasSwatchChartStep4": "Write the code under each row in pencil or with a liner.",
  "ideasSwatchChartStep5": "Use the Swatches tab to mark the markers you own and compare them with your chart.",
  "ideasOneColorTitle": "One colour family",
  "ideasOneColorSummary": "Draw a whole scene with four markers of a single colour family.",
  "ideasOneColorPractice": "Building depth with layers and with lighter and darker markers.",
  "ideasOneColorSubject": "A steaming cup of tea, or a small plant in a pot.",
  "ideasOneColorTip": "Pressing lightly does not make a marker paler. For light areas, use the lighter marker or the colourless blender.",
  "ideasOneColorStep1": "Pick a simple subject and mark in pencil where the light comes from.",
  "ideasOneColorStep2": "Cover everything except the highlights with the lightest marker.",
  "ideasOneColorStep3": "Add the base marker to the parts facing away from the light.",
  "ideasOneColorStep4": "Put the shadow marker in the darkest areas and blend the edges while they are wet.",
  "ideasOneColorStep5": "Keep the darkest marker for contact shadows and a few small details.",
  "ideasOneColorStep6": "To darken a tone without changing markers, add another layer of the same one once the first is dry.",
  "ideasTwoMarkersTitle": "Two-marker challenge",
  "ideasTwoMarkersSummary": "Just one light and one dark marker, nothing else.",
  "ideasTwoMarkersPractice": "Deciding what stays light and what goes dark before you start.",
  "ideasTwoMarkersSubject": "A mushroom, a sitting cat or a house with a roof.",
  "ideasTwoMarkersTip": "With only two markers, the white of the paper does a lot of the work: plan it from the start.",
  "ideasTwoMarkersStep1": "Make a small sketch first and decide which areas are light and which are dark.",
  "ideasTwoMarkersStep2": "Cover everything except the highlights with the light marker.",
  "ideasTwoMarkersStep3": "Add a second layer of the light marker on the half-shadows.",
  "ideasTwoMarkersStep4": "Use the dark marker only for the deepest shadows and a few details.",
  "ideasTwoMarkersStep5": "Leave the white paper in the brightest spots: it is your third tone.",
  "ideasTwoColorsTitle": "Two opposite colours",
  "ideasTwoColorsSummary": "Use two opposite colours and one grey, and keep them apart.",
  "ideasTwoColorsPractice": "Handling opposite colours, which turn muddy when they overlap.",
  "ideasTwoColorsSubject": "An orange on a blue cloth, or a red flower with green leaves.",
  "ideasTwoColorsTip": "Two opposite colours layered on top of each other turn grey or brown. Use that only where you want a dull shadow.",
  "ideasTwoColorsStep1": "Decide which areas belong to each colour and keep them as separate shapes.",
  "ideasTwoColorsStep2": "Colour the shapes of the first colour, from light to dark.",
  "ideasTwoColorsStep3": "Add its shadow while the first layer is still damp.",
  "ideasTwoColorsStep4": "Let it dry completely, then colour the shapes of the second colour, stopping at the border.",
  "ideasTwoColorsStep5": "Add the shadow of the second colour in the same way.",
  "ideasTwoColorsStep6": "Use the grey for shadows that cross from one colour to the other.",
  "ideasFiftyFiftyTitle": "Half warm, half cool",
  "ideasFiftyFiftySummary": "Split one drawing in two: warm colours on one side, cool ones on the other.",
  "ideasFiftyFiftyPractice": "Seeing how warm and cool colours change the mood of the same subject.",
  "ideasFiftyFiftySubject": "A face or a house split down the middle.",
  "ideasFiftyFiftyTip": "Wet edges run into each other: never let a wet warm area touch a wet cool one.",
  "ideasFiftyFiftyStep1": "Draw your subject and a line that splits it in two.",
  "ideasFiftyFiftyStep2": "Colour the warm half first, from light to dark.",
  "ideasFiftyFiftyStep3": "Add its shadows and let the whole half dry completely.",
  "ideasFiftyFiftyStep4": "Colour the cool half, stopping a millimetre short of the line, and close the gap once the other side is dry.",
  "ideasFiftyFiftyStep5": "Add the cool shadows in the same places as on the warm half.",
  "ideasFiftyFiftyStep6": "Use the grey only in the darkest spots, so both halves share it.",
  "ideasRandomColorsTitle": "Five random markers",
  "ideasRandomColorsSummary": "Draw anything with five markers picked at random from your collection.",
  "ideasRandomColorsPractice": "Making any colours work together by controlling light and dark.",
  "ideasRandomColorsSubject": "Anything you like: a bird, a teapot or a monster.",
  "ideasRandomColorsTip": "Light and dark matter more than colour: a drawing with clear contrast works with almost any colours.",
  "ideasRandomColorsStep1": "Look at the five markers above: they are sorted from lightest to darkest.",
  "ideasRandomColorsStep2": "Use the lightest one for the large lit areas.",
  "ideasRandomColorsStep3": "Use the middle ones for the main shapes.",
  "ideasRandomColorsStep4": "Keep the darkest one for shadows and outlines.",
  "ideasRandomColorsStep5": "If two colours clash, separate them with a thin line of white paper.",
  "ideasRandomColorsStep6": "Tap Other markers for a new draw and try again.",
  "ideasUnlikelyColorsTitle": "Unexpected colours",
  "ideasUnlikelyColorsSummary": "Colour a familiar object in colours that are not its own.",
  "ideasUnlikelyColorsPractice": "Keeping light and dark right so the object still reads, whatever its colour.",
  "ideasUnlikelyColorsSubject": "A banana, a dog or your own trainer.",
  "ideasUnlikelyColorsTip": "If you squint and the drawing still reads, the light and dark are right, whatever the colours.",
  "ideasUnlikelyColorsStep1": "Draw the object from life or from a photo and notice where its light and dark areas are.",
  "ideasUnlikelyColorsStep2": "Use the lightest marker where the real object is lightest.",
  "ideasUnlikelyColorsStep3": "Use the base where the real object has its middle tones.",
  "ideasUnlikelyColorsStep4": "Use the shadow marker where the real object is darkest.",
  "ideasUnlikelyColorsStep5": "Add the accent in one small area to catch the eye.",
  "ideasPastelTitle": "Soft pastels",
  "ideasPastelSummary": "Colour a soft, airy drawing with light markers and plenty of white paper.",
  "ideasPastelPractice": "Light, even layers and keeping the white of the paper.",
  "ideasPastelSubject": "A cupcake, a bunny or a bunch of flowers.",
  "ideasPastelTip": "Pressing lightly does not make a marker paler: choose a lighter marker instead.",
  "ideasPastelStep1": "Pick a gentle subject and keep the pencil lines very light.",
  "ideasPastelStep2": "Decide where the paper stays white: the highlights and part of the background.",
  "ideasPastelStep3": "Colour each area with one light marker in a single even layer, in small circles.",
  "ideasPastelStep4": "Make the shadow with a second layer of the same marker, not with a darker colour.",
  "ideasPastelStep5": "Use the slightly deeper marker only for the smallest shadows.",
  "ideasPastelStep6": "Soften any hard edge with the colourless blender.",
  "ideasSkinTonesTitle": "Skin with four markers",
  "ideasSkinTonesSummary": "Colour a face with a light tone, a base, a shadow and a blush.",
  "ideasSkinTonesPractice": "Soft skin blending and warm shadows on a face.",
  "ideasSkinTonesSubject": "A face in three-quarter view, lit from one side.",
  "ideasSkinTonesTip": "Skin shadows are warm: avoid greys, they make the face look dirty.",
  "ideasSkinTonesStep1": "Draw the face lightly and mark the shadow shapes: under the brow, beside the nose, under the lip and along the jaw.",
  "ideasSkinTonesStep2": "Cover all the skin with the light marker, leaving the brightest spots of the forehead, nose and cheek as white paper.",
  "ideasSkinTonesStep3": "While it is damp, add the blush on the cheeks, the tip of the nose and the ears.",
  "ideasSkinTonesStep4": "Put the base on the shadow shapes and blend their edges with the light marker.",
  "ideasSkinTonesStep5": "Use the shadow marker only where shapes meet: under the chin, inside the ear and around the nostrils.",
  "ideasSkinTonesStep6": "Soften any hard line with the light marker rather than the colourless blender, so the skin does not go blotchy.",
  "ideasMidnightTitle": "Moonlit night",
  "ideasMidnightSummary": "A small house at night, lit by the moon and one warm window.",
  "ideasMidnightPractice": "Planning a night scene: cool shadows and a few clean, warm lights.",
  "ideasMidnightSubject": "A small house at night with one lit window.",
  "ideasMidnightTip": "Do not let the blue and the yellow touch while either is wet: together they turn green.",
  "ideasMidnightStep1": "Before you start, decide where the lights are: the moon and the window stay white paper.",
  "ideasMidnightStep2": "Colour the window and a soft glow around it with the yellow, and let it dry completely.",
  "ideasMidnightStep3": "Cover the sky and the lit walls with the light blue, working away from the moon.",
  "ideasMidnightStep4": "Add the base blue to everything in shadow, a few millimetres away from the yellow glow.",
  "ideasMidnightStep5": "Darken the sky towards the top and the shadows under the roof and the trees.",
  "ideasMidnightStep6": "Use the darkest marker for the deepest shadows and, once dry, add a few stars with white gel.",
  "ideasUnderwaterTitle": "Under the sea",
  "ideasUnderwaterSummary": "Sunbeams in blue water around a small fish.",
  "ideasUnderwaterPractice": "Lifting rays of light with the colourless blender.",
  "ideasUnderwaterSubject": "A small fish swimming above a coral.",
  "ideasUnderwaterTip": "Try the blender rays on a scrap first: they only work while the ink is still slightly damp.",
  "ideasUnderwaterStep1": "Colour the fish and the coral first with the accent and let them dry.",
  "ideasUnderwaterStep2": "Cover the water with the light blue, stopping just short of the fish.",
  "ideasUnderwaterStep3": "Add the base blue to the lower half and blend it upwards while it is wet.",
  "ideasUnderwaterStep4": "Darken the bottom and the area behind the fish so it stands out.",
  "ideasUnderwaterStep5": "While the blues are still damp, pull diagonal rays down from the surface with the colourless blender: it pushes the ink away.",
  "ideasUnderwaterStep6": "Add the darkest marker at the very bottom and, once dry, finish with tiny bubbles in white gel.",
  "ideasGalaxyTitle": "Galaxy",
  "ideasGalaxySummary": "Blend glowing nebula colours into a dark sky and finish with stars.",
  "ideasGalaxyPractice": "Blending wet into wet, and lifting ink to make stars.",
  "ideasGalaxySubject": "A nebula over the silhouette of a mountain.",
  "ideasGalaxyTip": "Keep the big stars to a handful: too many flatten the depth.",
  "ideasGalaxyStep1": "Use marker paper that does not bleed through and put a sheet underneath: this exercise takes a lot of ink.",
  "ideasGalaxyStep2": "Start with the lightest nebula colours in soft, irregular clouds.",
  "ideasGalaxyStep3": "While they are still wet, blend the middle colour into their edges.",
  "ideasGalaxyStep4": "Fill the rest of the sky with the darkest markers, working towards the clouds, not over them.",
  "ideasGalaxyStep5": "Dab the colourless blender on the dark areas to lift little dots of ink: they will be distant stars.",
  "ideasGalaxyStep6": "Once dry, add a few bright stars and a cross-shaped sparkle with white gel.",
  "ideasNeonTitle": "Neon sign",
  "ideasNeonSummary": "Make a sign glow with a white core and a dark background.",
  "ideasNeonPractice": "Glow: a white core, a bright halo and a dark background.",
  "ideasNeonSubject": "The word OPEN, or a heart drawn as a neon tube.",
  "ideasNeonTip": "Fluorescent markers are see-through: use them on white, never over dark ink.",
  "ideasNeonStep1": "Draw a simple word or shape in pencil with thick strokes.",
  "ideasNeonStep2": "Leave a thin line of white paper in the middle of each stroke: that is the hot core.",
  "ideasNeonStep3": "Colour the rest of the stroke with the light version of your neon colour.",
  "ideasNeonStep4": "Flick the bright colour outwards from the stroke to make a soft halo.",
  "ideasNeonStep5": "When the halo is dry, fill the background with the darkest marker, stopping a few millimetres from the halo.",
  "ideasNeonStep6": "Blend that gap with the dark marker so the glow fades into the night.",
  "ideasSepiaPhotoTitle": "Old sepia photo",
  "ideasSepiaPhotoSummary": "Draw a portrait or a building as if it were an old brown photograph.",
  "ideasSepiaPhotoPractice": "Working with one warm family of browns, like a light-and-dark study in colour.",
  "ideasSepiaPhotoSubject": "An old house, a cowboy hat or a portrait of a grandparent.",
  "ideasSepiaPhotoTip": "Avoid black and cool greys: they break the warm sepia look.",
  "ideasSepiaPhotoStep1": "Pencil the subject and frame it with a border that stays white paper.",
  "ideasSepiaPhotoStep2": "Cover everything except the brightest spots with the lightest brown.",
  "ideasSepiaPhotoStep3": "Build the middle tones with the base brown.",
  "ideasSepiaPhotoStep4": "Add the shadows with the shadow brown, blending the edges while they are wet.",
  "ideasSepiaPhotoStep5": "Use the darkest brown instead of black for the deepest shadows.",
  "ideasSepiaPhotoStep6": "Darken the corners of the picture slightly with the warm grey to make it look old.",
  "ideasSunsetTitle": "Sunset sky",
  "ideasSunsetSummary": "Blend a sky from yellow to violet and add a dark silhouette.",
  "ideasSunsetPractice": "Blending neighbouring colours wet into wet over a large area.",
  "ideasSunsetSubject": "A sunset over the sea with a palm tree or a city skyline in silhouette.",
  "ideasSunsetTip": "Only blend neighbouring colours: yellow next to violet turns brownish while wet.",
  "ideasSunsetStep1": "Pencil the horizon and the silhouette; keep the sun as a circle of white paper.",
  "ideasSunsetStep2": "Start at the sun: colour a wide band of yellow around it.",
  "ideasSunsetStep3": "While the yellow is wet, add the orange above it and blend the join.",
  "ideasSunsetStep4": "Carry on upwards with the pink, blending each band into the last while it is wet.",
  "ideasSunsetStep5": "Finish the top of the sky with the violet.",
  "ideasSunsetStep6": "When the sky is dry, fill the silhouette with the darkest marker in flat, even strokes.",
  "ideasSpringTitle": "Spring blossom",
  "ideasSpringSummary": "A branch of cherry blossom with soft pink petals.",
  "ideasSpringPractice": "Delicate petals: light layers and soft edges.",
  "ideasSpringSubject": "A branch with five or six blossoms and a few leaves.",
  "ideasSpringTip": "Petals are thin: two light layers look more delicate than one dark one.",
  "ideasSpringStep1": "Draw the branch and the flowers lightly, with five petals each.",
  "ideasSpringStep2": "Colour each petal with the light pink, leaving a thin white edge on some of them.",
  "ideasSpringStep3": "Add the base pink near the centre of each flower, flicking outwards.",
  "ideasSpringStep4": "Touch the deepest pink only in the centre and where petals overlap.",
  "ideasSpringStep5": "Colour the leaves and the young shoots with the green.",
  "ideasSpringStep6": "Shade the underside of the leaves with the dark green.",
  "ideasAutumnTitle": "Autumn leaves",
  "ideasAutumnSummary": "A still life of fallen leaves in warm, glowing colours.",
  "ideasAutumnPractice": "Blending neighbouring warm colours and keeping the veins light.",
  "ideasAutumnSubject": "Three fallen leaves on a table: oak, maple and chestnut.",
  "ideasAutumnTip": "Leave the veins as a lighter line: it is easier than drawing them on top later.",
  "ideasAutumnStep1": "Draw the leaves and their main veins in light pencil.",
  "ideasAutumnStep2": "Colour the lightest leaf with the yellow, going around the veins so they stay light.",
  "ideasAutumnStep3": "Colour the next leaf with the light orange and blend a little yellow into its edges while wet.",
  "ideasAutumnStep4": "Colour the third leaf with the base and blend the light orange into its tip.",
  "ideasAutumnStep5": "Add the red along the edges and in a few patches, as if the leaf were changing colour.",
  "ideasAutumnStep6": "Shade where the leaves overlap and where they touch the table with the dark orange.",
  "ideasWinterTitle": "Snowy cabin",
  "ideasWinterSummary": "A cabin in the snow where the white is the paper itself.",
  "ideasWinterPractice": "Drawing snow with blue-grey shadows and lots of white paper.",
  "ideasWinterSubject": "A wooden cabin with snow on the roof and one lit window.",
  "ideasWinterTip": "Do not shade snow with warm grey: blue-greys make it look cold and clean.",
  "ideasWinterStep1": "Pencil the cabin and where the snow ends: the snow is white paper, so plan it first.",
  "ideasWinterStep2": "Colour the window with the yellow and let it dry.",
  "ideasWinterStep3": "Colour the wooden walls with the brown without touching the snow on the roof.",
  "ideasWinterStep4": "Shade the snow with the light blue-grey only where the light does not reach.",
  "ideasWinterStep5": "Add the darker blue-grey under the roof and in the footprints, blending it into the light one.",
  "ideasWinterStep6": "Use the darkest marker for the door and the deepest shadows and, once dry, add falling snow with white gel.",
  "ideasChristmasTitle": "Christmas baubles",
  "ideasChristmasSummary": "Shiny red baubles hanging from a green fir branch.",
  "ideasChristmasPractice": "Round, shiny shapes with a kept highlight and reflections.",
  "ideasChristmasSubject": "Two red baubles hanging from a fir branch.",
  "ideasChristmasTip": "Draw the branch after the baubles, so the green never runs into the red.",
  "ideasChristmasStep1": "Draw the baubles and plan a window-shaped highlight on each one.",
  "ideasChristmasStep2": "Cover each bauble with the light red, going around the highlight.",
  "ideasChristmasStep3": "Add the base red in a ring that follows the round shape, leaving the middle lighter.",
  "ideasChristmasStep4": "Darken the edge furthest from the light, keeping a thin lighter rim for the reflection.",
  "ideasChristmasStep5": "Flick the needles of the branch with the green, from the stem outwards.",
  "ideasChristmasStep6": "Add the dark green where the needles overlap and, once dry, a white gel sparkle on each bauble.",
  "ideasHalloweenTitle": "Halloween pumpkin",
  "ideasHalloweenSummary": "A glowing carved pumpkin in a dark violet night.",
  "ideasHalloweenPractice": "Light coming from inside an object, against a dark background.",
  "ideasHalloweenSubject": "A carved pumpkin with a candle inside, on a doorstep.",
  "ideasHalloweenTip": "Lay the dark background last and slowly near the edge: dark ink runs into light areas.",
  "ideasHalloweenStep1": "Draw the pumpkin and its carved face; the inside of the face will be the brightest part.",
  "ideasHalloweenStep2": "Colour the carved holes with the light orange, leaving a white core in the middle.",
  "ideasHalloweenStep3": "Colour the pumpkin with the base orange, following its ribs with curved strokes.",
  "ideasHalloweenStep4": "Shade the grooves between the ribs and the side away from the glow.",
  "ideasHalloweenStep5": "Colour the stalk with the green.",
  "ideasHalloweenStep6": "When everything is dry, fill the night with the dark violet, starting a few millimetres away from the pumpkin.",
